  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 314 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nBreezy east-northeasterly winds this morning in the wake of the\noutflow boundary have significantly impacted the ability to \nheat up through early afternoon. This has particularly been true\nacross far northern/northeastern IL, where temperatures as of 1\npm ranged from the mid to upper 70s near the lake, to the mid \n80s farther inland. This prompted us to cancel the Heat Advisory\nearlier for Cook, Lake (IL), McHenry and Boone counties. In \nspite of this, expect temperatures to spike into the upper 80s \nto low 90s late this afternoon as the low-level flow gradually \nveers back to the south-southwest. This could result in a couple\nhour period of heat indices reaching 95-100 outside the current\nHeat Advisory. Conversely, slightly warmer temperatures and \nhigher humidity within the Heat Advisory area will support peak \nheat indices around (or a bit above) 105 degrees through early \nevening. \n\nIn addition to stunting the heat across northeastern IL, The \nbreezy east-northeasterly outflow winds this morning have \ncreated rather choppy continues along the southern Lake Michigan\nshore. Wave heights as high as 4.5' were reported from a buoy \noff the northern Cook county shore. Waves of this height are \nhazardous to small craft and swimmers at area beaches across \nnortheastern IL. For this reason, we hoisted a Small Craft \nadvisory and Beach Hazard statement through 7 PM this evening \nfor the northeastern IL Lake Michigan shore. Easing and veering \nflow should allow these conditions to subside into this evening.\n\nThe outflow boundary from this morning's convection across \nsouthern WI has settled into areas south of the Kankakee river \nvalley across eastern IL and northwestern IN early this \nafternoon. However, it bends back northward across far \nnorthwestern IL. The airmass south and west of this boundary \nremains quite unstable (per the 18Z DVN RAOB). This has recently\nsupported the development of some scattered strong storms west-\nnorthwest of the area in closer proximity to a surface cold \nfront and approaching mid-level impulse. It is currently unclear\nas to how long this activity will hold together, owing to \nsuboptimal deep-layer shear. Nevertheless, if this activity is \nable to develop a formidable cold pool, a cluster of \nsoutheastward shift storms could approach parts of far northern \nIL this evening after 7 pm. Confidence in this holding together \nremains low at this time. \n\nIf the activity to northwest dissipates, there remains a play \nin which we experience renewed development with the front mid to\nlate this evening into the overnight as falling heights aloft \noverspread the frontal boundary in response to the main \nmid/upper-level ridge axis retrograding westward and building \nnorthward across the Missouri Valley. If this occurs, the \nfavored placement would be across northeastern IL into \nnorthwestern IN from 9pm through 3 am. While not looking to be \nmuch of a severe threat, some locally strong wind gusts could \naccompany the storms, particularly if they form into a \nsoutheastward shifting cluster.\n\nThe progression of the front is expected to continue into \ncentral IL and IN on Saturday. Very warm and humid conditions \nahead of this front could result in temperatures (heat indices) \ntopping 90 (100) again across my far southern counties, but at \nthis time another Heat Advisory is not planned for the area. \nOtherwise, behind the front, it will be turn somewhat cooler and\na bit less humid Saturday afternoon, but a period of low \nstratus may be slow to improve during the afternoon. \nTemperatures and humidity will continue to improve into Sunday.\n\nA period of moderately strong northerly winds down the full \nfetch of the lake should result in an increase in waves later \nSaturday through early Sunday. It is possible that a beach \nhazard statement may be needed for dangerous swim conditions for\na part of this weekend, which is the closing weekend of the \nbeach season.\n\nDry and seasonably warm weather is expected Labor Day. \nTemperatures will warm some and humidity will be on the increase\nTuesday into Wednesday in the warm air advection regime ahead \nof the next shortwave. An associated surface cold front could \nbring some showers and thunderstorms with it midweek before \nanother bout of somewhat cooler, more seasonable, temperatures \nfilter in behind the front to close out the short work week.\n\nKJB",
